    <statement><p>The graph of <m>f'</m> (<alert>not</alert> <m>f</m>) is given below.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap2Sec5__Q21_pg-image-1.png" width="50%">
<shortdescription>Graph of a function</shortdescription>
</image>
<p><em>(Note that this is a graph of <m>f'</m>, not a graph of <m>f</m>.)</em></p>
<p>At which of the marked values of <m>x</m> is</p>
<p><alert>A.</alert>
<m>f(x)</m> greatest?  <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert>B.</alert>
<m>f(x)</m> least? <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><alert>C.</alert>
<m>f'(x)</m> greatest? <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><alert>D.</alert>
<m>f'(x)</m> least? <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p><alert>E.</alert>
<m>f''(x)</m> greatest? <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0005"/></p>
<p><alert>F.</alert>
<m>f''(x)</m> least? <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="8" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{x6}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{x1}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{x3}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{x5}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{x6}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{x4}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><alert>A,B.</alert>
Since <m>f'</m> is everywhere positive, <m>f</m> is everywhere increasing.
Hence the greatest value of <m>f</m> is at x6 and the least value
of <m>f</m> is at x1.</p>
<p><alert>C,D.</alert>
Directly from the graph, we see that <m>f'</m>
is greatest at x3 and least at x5.</p>
<p><alert>E,F.</alert>
Since <m>f''</m> gives the
slope of the graph of <m>f'</m>, <m>f''</m> is greatest where <m>f'</m> is
rising most rapidly, namely at x6, and <m>f''</m> is least where
<m>f'</m> is falling most rapidly, namely at x4.</p>
</solution>
